1
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:17,595
ANN: As far as we have been able to know, we really didn't get

2
00:00:17,595 --> 00:00:23,313
very far. We only scratched the surface as far as the history of our

3
00:00:23,313 --> 00:00:26,720
church and of the Presbyterian Church in Western Pennsylvania.

4
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:33,069
But I hope that out of these classes we can have a deeper appreciation

5
00:00:33,069 --> 00:00:39,418
of the life that our frontier forefathers lived in this area, their way of

6
00:00:39,418 --> 00:00:44,860
life, their tremendous interest in religion, and also their interest in education.

7
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:49,660
ANN: This is one of the things that I've tried to emphasize through each session,

8
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:56,980
that the Presbyterian forefathers were

9
00:00:56,980 --> 00:01:03,740
interested not only in their own religion, but in educating their children and providing

10
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:08,040
for them the opportunities to go on to a better life.

11
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:16,660
ANN: And we talked about George Marshall, especially before, and how he was

12
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:22,100
influenced by very fine teachers in his early life, and through that established that

13
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:27,620
special academy as an early part of his activity in relationship to Bethel Church,

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,160
because it has been significant to me that this church has survived because of

15
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:39,140
its contacts and its influences by other churches.

16
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:46,380
ANN: Last week, we sort of left the church and went elsewhere, took the group

17
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:53,540
and went to Mingo Presbyterian Church, which enabled us to touch hands with history

18
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:58,360
of the past, and the relationship that Bethel Church had with the Mingo congregation

19
00:01:58,430 --> 00:02:01,030
during the period [unclear].

20
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:05,690
ANN: This week we're going to touch in again with another congregation.

21
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:13,350
ANN: This time with the history of Cross Creek Presbyterian Church, which very definitely was

22
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:18,990
interwoven with our own history during the pastorate of Dr. George Marshall.

23
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:25,110
And the one man that I think can really open this picture to us,

24
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:30,870
here with us today, to give us his history of the Cross Creek Church,

25
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:36,690
and also some of the other materials that he can so ably provide for

26
00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:37,910
us as background.

27
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:44,570
ANN: Before I introduce him, I would like to state that one of the most

28
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:45,770
important things that Dr.

29
00:02:46,070 --> 00:02:50,970
Marshall considered to the life of the church was his library.

30
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:57,110
He loved books, and he spent most of his money, what little he had

31
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:01,250
extra, in accumulating an outstanding library.

32
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:06,450
And he lamented the fact that so many churches spend so much money on

33
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:11,450
the beautification of their church and so little to provide books for the minister

34
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:13,790
who needed that for research and study.

35
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:21,770
ANN: We have with us a man who also loves books, who has written a

36
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:27,590
history of the Cross Creek Church, has an extensive library of his own, and

37
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:33,790
is most gracious in sharing with us some of the books that are precious

38
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:39,390
to him. And because we have a fine library, I hope all of you

39
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:43,450
will take advantage of it. Under the supervision of Mrs.

40
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:49,790
Tanner, I'm going to present to her the book which Mr.

41
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:57,160
Alvin Dinsmore White made and has put in our library, and in the front

42
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:59,760
has inserted the following.

43
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:06,660
ANN: This volume, History of the Presbytery of Washington, is presented to the library at

44
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:12,240
the historical collection of the Bethel Presbyterian Church by Alvin Dinsmore White of Hickory,

45
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:19,420
Pennsylvania, in loving memory of his great, great, great grandfather, James Dinsmore, a noted

46
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:22,960
churchman and once an elder in the Bethel Presbyterian Church.

47
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:28,180
He was one of the first three recorded in our history at the time

48
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:33,180
that the church was still meeting at the log cabin home of Oliver Miller.

49
00:04:33,180 --> 00:04:39,920
ANN: And then it goes on to say that there is information here concerning

50
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:47,720
James Dinsmore, 1742 to 1817, served as elder in Bethel Church until

51
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:55,580
1795; John Dinsmore, 1779 to 1858, served as elder for many years

52
00:04:55,580 --> 00:05:02,340
in Upper Buffalo Church; James Dinsmore, 1806 to 1870, who served as elder in

53
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:03,680
the Cross Creek district.

54
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:13,200
ANN: Thank you. I hope that you will make that book available to us

55
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:18,160
through the library. You might be interested to know that Mrs.

56
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:25,080
Moser, in her fine talk on revivalism in western Pennsylvania — use them, they're

57
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:27,500
quite accessible for her.

58
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:34,100
We have a few other books on the shelf that are referred to in

59
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:37,680
this book, so that we'll get things together, so that you can find them

60
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:40,420
wherever we put them.

61
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:42,920
ANN: Thank you. Thank you very much.

62
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:48,360
And now, without any further speech or debate,

63
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:57,320
to you, Alvin Dinsmore White, who has a long history and love and interest

64
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:59,020
in the Presbyterian Church.

65
00:05:59,300 --> 00:06:03,940
He grew up as a boy in the Cross Creek Church, was an elder

66
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:11,140
in the [unclear] Cross Creek Church, and also was not only a churchman but

67
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:16,980
an educator. He taught school, beginning in a one-room schoolhouse, then he

68
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:24,820
taught in a high school, and then became supervisor of elementary education for various

69
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,660
districts in the Avella school district.

70
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:32,960
ANN: He is an author, a historian, a teacher, and a devoted churchman.

71
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:41,540
WHITE: Thank you, Mrs. Conner.

72
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:49,000
After such an introduction, I wish I could bring you a message this morning

73
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:53,460
that would make a match with what Mrs. Conner has said.

74
00:06:54,460 --> 00:07:02,140
WHITE: We were here last February, which time I tried to show the pictures of

75
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:07,960
some historic characters of the area, in which I have been very much interested

76
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:09,400
in recent years.

77
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:14,680
WHITE: I don't know whether this is the same group I addressed at that time

78
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:20,060
or not. I think some of you are, but following that meeting we have

79
00:07:20,060 --> 00:07:23,740
become very well acquainted with Mr. and Mrs.

80
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:30,420
John Conner, and found their deep interest in church history in general and the

81
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:32,520
history of the Bethel Church in particular.

82
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:40,260
WHITE: I do not like to start on a note of sarcasm, but I feel

83
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:47,240
like saying that we come from a church in which at present the word

84
00:07:47,240 --> 00:07:54,320
“church history” is a dirty word, and any mention of the religious heritage

85
00:07:54,320 --> 00:08:01,040
which our community has over where we live seems to be almost a heresy.

86
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:06,860
So it is quite refreshing, I assure you, to come this morning into a

87
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:14,320
church and into a group where I can indulge my interest in church history

88
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:18,520
without being reprimanded for the offence.

89
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:26,560
WHITE: Mrs. Conner, in a recent communication, sent me an outline which she asked me

90
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:31,940
to follow if I could, and I will do so as religiously as I

91
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:37,780
can. It was along the line of some things I had intended to speak

92
00:08:37,780 --> 00:08:44,660
to you about anyway, since she invited us to come here some weeks ago.

93
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:53,560
WHITE: Among the things that she asked, the first on the agenda was to say

94
00:08:53,560 --> 00:09:01,060
something about the life of my great-great-great-grandfather, James Dinsmore, to whom she has already

95
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:03,920
referred in her introductory remarks.

96
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:14,420
WHITE: James Dinsmore was a Scottish-Irishman about whom we do not know as much

97
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:20,040
as we would like to know, being still a member of the Dinsmore clan.

98
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:27,100
WHITE: My middle name being Dinsmore, I can't evade the fact that I belong to

99
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:28,360
the Dinsmore clan.

100
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:36,520
WHITE: We have annual family reunions, and they are very enjoyable

101
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:40,560
as we get together with others who are descended from this man.

102
00:09:42,090 --> 00:09:47,170
WHITE: I have jotted down here some of the things that we do know about

103
00:09:47,170 --> 00:09:51,930
James Dinsmore, and I'm going to speak first about his personal life.

104
00:09:52,130 --> 00:09:59,050
He was born in County Antrim, near Belfast, on April 26, 1742, and came

105
00:09:59,050 --> 00:10:05,170
to America with two or three brothers in 1760 or 1761.

106
00:10:05,870 --> 00:10:13,470
WHITE: And in the following winter, probably in the winter of 1761 and 1762, he

107
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:21,290
and his brother Robert crossed the Allegheny Mountains by making snowshoes of splits 75

108
00:10:21,290 --> 00:10:22,950
inches in size.

109
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:27,610
WHITE: When he came here he settled near Pittsburgh, where he lived for a number

110
00:10:27,610 --> 00:10:34,093
of years. In 1772 he settled on Miller's Run in the present South Fayette

111
00:10:34,093 --> 00:10:40,230
Township — that is, to the southwest a couple of miles from Bridgeville.

112
00:10:40,370 --> 00:10:45,090
But the patent for his land there — the Land of Canaan it was

113
00:10:45,090 --> 00:10:47,810
called — was not given until 1786.

114
00:10:49,730 --> 00:10:54,390
WHITE: He frequently went back east over the mountains to obtain salt, and during the

115
00:10:54,390 --> 00:11:00,530
years of the Revolutionary War James Dinsmore served in various capacities, for at least

116
00:11:00,530 --> 00:11:06,750
we know that a James Dinsmore is given credit for certain war services, and

117
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:12,970
descendants of our James Dinsmore have joined the DAR and SAR on his war

118
00:11:12,970 --> 00:11:13,470
record.

119
00:11:15,050 --> 00:11:21,990
WHITE: James Dinsmore was married at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, in 1776 to Miss Rebecca Walker, and

120
00:11:21,990 --> 00:11:25,370
it would appear that they lived, at least during a part of the war

121
00:11:25,370 --> 00:11:28,030
years, in York or Adams County.

122
00:11:29,070 --> 00:11:32,166
WHITE: After the purchase of this tract of land — the Land of Canaan —

123
00:11:32,430 --> 00:11:36,690
however, they appear to have lived constantly in South Fayette Township.

124
00:11:37,470 --> 00:11:42,740
WHITE: They belonged to the Bethel Presbyterian Church, which James Dinsmore served as elder.

125
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:50,120
In 1791 Rebecca Walker Dinsmore died, and her body was brought for burial to

126
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:54,500
the old graveyard here at Bethel. But her grave has never been marked.

127
00:11:55,300 --> 00:12:01,620
WHITE: One day, several years ago, other descendants of James Dinsmore came — we came

128
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:08,500
over here to Bethel and we searched the cemetery over, but found no record,

129
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:11,000
no marker to her grave.

130
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:17,760
WHITE: And I believe that we were told at the headquarters and cemetery that there

131
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:19,580
was no record of her burial.

132
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:23,680
But the tradition of the family is that her body was brought here for

133
00:12:23,680 --> 00:12:26,140
burial. On the day of her funeral, the rough coffin

134
00:12:26,380 --> 00:12:31,340
was strapped to a pair of farm wagon wheels and thus transported to

135
00:12:31,340 --> 00:12:36,060
the graveyard, while James Dinsmore, the husband, and some of his neighbors followed on

136
00:12:36,060 --> 00:12:36,640
horseback.

137
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:42,600
WHITE: His son John, then a lad of about 12 years, I think, wanted to

138
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:47,360
follow his mother to her last resting place, but he was told that he

139
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:47,860
could not.

140
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:53,060
WHITE: He intended to walk, so he carefully turned back home.

141
00:12:54,360 --> 00:13:02,280
WHITE: In 1795 James Dinsmore sold his farm in South Fayette Township, because it

142
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:06,760
was too far from church — about seven miles — and he purchased a

143
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:12,720
tract known as Huntington Plantation in Canton Township, about six miles from Washington and

144
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:14,500
three miles from Buffalo Village.

145
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:21,080
WHITE: In this new location he transferred his membership in 1795 — I think the

146
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:24,700
records of the church here show that — from the Bethel Church to the

147
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:29,600
Upper Buffalo Church at Buffalo Village, and soon he was elected an elder there,

148
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:33,460
serving that office until his death in 1817.

149
00:13:35,530 --> 00:13:43,190
WHITE: In 1800, in a double wedding ceremony, James Dinsmore

150
00:13:43,190 --> 00:13:47,150
was married to his third wife, Elizabeth Carr.

151
00:13:48,290 --> 00:13:55,050
WHITE: And in the same ceremony, his son, John Dinsmore, was united to her sister,

152
00:13:55,290 --> 00:14:01,270
Jane Carr. And then that was a sufficient tie between the Dinsmore and the

153
00:14:01,270 --> 00:14:07,970
Carr families. Three years later a daughter, Anna Dinsmore, was married to James Carr,

154
00:14:08,210 --> 00:14:10,350
a brother of Elizabeth and Jane.

155
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:15,970
So there were three intermarriages there in a period of three years, and it

156
00:14:15,970 --> 00:14:22,790
is rather notable that the father and the son of this Dinsmore family married

157
00:14:22,790 --> 00:14:23,210
sisters.

158
00:14:24,690 --> 00:14:29,270
WHITE: Their home in the new location was probably a log house, but in 1808

159
00:14:29,270 --> 00:14:35,570
and 1809 bricks were burnt on Huntington Plantation, and in 1810 a large brick

160
00:14:35,570 --> 00:14:38,230
house was built — 14 rooms.

161
00:14:38,970 --> 00:14:43,470
WHITE: The home of the two families, James Dinsmore and his wife, his son John

162
00:14:43,470 --> 00:14:44,710
and his wife and family.

163
00:14:45,370 --> 00:14:48,770
And it was the home of James Dinsmore for the rest of his life,

164
00:14:48,810 --> 00:14:50,430
and he died in his own house.

165
00:14:50,430 --> 00:14:57,770
It is still standing, and is the home of another great-great-great grandson of James

166
00:14:57,770 --> 00:15:01,350
Dinsmore, Malcolm Dinsmore, and his wife and family.

167
00:15:01,870 --> 00:15:04,450
The whole property is still in the Dinsmore name.

168
00:15:05,490 --> 00:15:10,370
WHITE: That is, I think, sufficient about all we know actually about the personal life

169
00:15:10,370 --> 00:15:11,330
of James Dinsmore.

170
00:15:12,390 --> 00:15:19,310
WHITE: I have two quotations now that I want to give you from [unclear]

171
00:15:19,310 --> 00:15:23,470
concerning his saintliness and godliness.

172
00:15:25,750 --> 00:15:33,240
WHITE: This says that he was noted for his saintliness and as an eminently pious

173
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:38,400
man of God. He is spoken of as the burning and shining light, a

174
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:41,300
man wonderfully full of the spirit of prayer.

175
00:15:41,800 --> 00:15:49,180
When the Bethel Presbyterian Church was organized, he, James Brice, and James Kirkpatrick —

176
00:15:49,180 --> 00:15:54,600
three men, I believe the name James in that direction — became its first

177
00:15:54,600 --> 00:16:00,040
ruling elders, and he continued in this capacity until he was dismissed to the

178
00:16:00,040 --> 00:16:05,260
Upper Buffalo Church in 1795, where he served as an elder the rest of

179
00:16:05,260 --> 00:16:05,660
his life.

180
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:11,540
WHITE: As an indication of his standing in the early Presbyterian Church, he was appointed

181
00:16:11,540 --> 00:16:16,560
to the Board of Commissioners of the Western Missionary Society of the Synod of

182
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:18,220
Pittsburgh in 1804.

183
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:24,060
WHITE: Another tribute comes from a grandson, the Reverend James H.

184
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:31,680
Dinsmore, a graduate of W&J College, and a man who, although he was not

185
00:16:31,680 --> 00:16:36,300
a family man — he was never married — was familiar with the family

186
00:16:36,300 --> 00:16:41,100
tradition. And he says this concerning his grandfather, James Dinsmore:

187
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:46,920
WHITE: Grandfather was a farmer and well-to-do, as were most of his male descendants.

188
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:50,600
WHITE: I don't know whether that applies to all his male descendants.

189
00:16:50,600 --> 00:16:55,640
I couldn't vouch for that, I felt it a little bit, but he was

190
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:59,260
a large man and very corpulent in his old age.

191
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:03,940
He weighed, according to the family tradition, 300 pounds.

192
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:06,240
He must have been quite a man physically.

193
00:17:07,020 --> 00:17:11,460
WHITE: For nearly 50 years he was an honored elder in the Presbyterian Church.

194
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:17,075
For many years — and here's an error — at Montour or Miller's Run,

195
00:17:17,780 --> 00:17:22,780
Allegheny County, Pennsylvania. His grandson should have known better than that.

196
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:29,200
But that statement about Miller's Run — plenty of Dinsmores went up to the

197
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:36,220
old Miller's Run Presbyterian Church in Cecil Township, a congregation which is now non-existent.

198
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:40,740
But the old church is still there, the old building, and the graveyard is

199
00:17:40,740 --> 00:17:41,600
very historic.

200
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:47,480
WHITE: But some of his people have looked all over that old graveyard for some

201
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:51,180
of the Dinsmore graves, but that is apparently an error, because he was an

202
00:17:51,180 --> 00:17:58,140
elder first here, and then for many years at Upper Buffalo — a contemporary

203
00:17:58,140 --> 00:18:04,020
with Dr. McMillan, the founder of Jefferson College, and who was also his pastor.

204
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:10,840
That statement also was a little bit of an error, because while I have

205
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:13,300
no doubt he was very well acquainted with Dr.

206
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:14,420
John McMillan, and yet, except that [he was] the organizing pastor here, I don't

207
00:18:14,420 --> 00:18:15,540
believe John McMillan would have been his pastor by any chance in the world.

208
00:19:05,080 --> 00:19:06,200
WHITE: For the last 25 or 30 years of his life he was an elder

209
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:07,320
in the Upper Buffalo Church in Washington County with those eminent preachers and pioneers,

210
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:08,440
Joseph Smith, Thomas Marquis, John Anderson, and he participated in that wonderful revival in

211
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:09,560
the early part of this century — the great revival of 1802 — when

212
00:19:09,560 --> 00:19:10,680
the great camp meeting was held at the Upper Buffalo Church, attended, some say,

213
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:11,800
by 10,000 people who came there from a radius of 100 miles or so,

214
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:12,480
camped on the grounds, and had that great evangelistic [unclear].

215
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:14,540
WHITE: His memory is still precious, and he has left his descendants the unspeakable blessing

216
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:23,010
of an eminently good and very influential Christian, whom the Church failed not to

217
00:19:23,010 --> 00:19:30,790
honor. The records of Presbytery show during James Dinsmore's lifetime that he was in

218
00:19:30,870 --> 00:19:38,690
attendance at at least 21 meetings of these — both Synod of Pittsburgh and

219
00:19:38,690 --> 00:19:43,410
the Presbytery of Redstone,

220
00:19:47,890 --> 00:19:55,690
I guess. His son John Dinsmore, my great-great-grandfather, followed his father

221
00:19:55,690 --> 00:20:00,110
as an elder in the Upper Buffalo Church, and he served there until his

222
00:20:00,110 --> 00:20:01,550
death in 1858.

223
00:20:02,970 --> 00:20:07,810
WHITE: What I'm trying to get at in the enumeration here of the good qualities

224
00:20:07,810 --> 00:20:13,070
of James Dinsmore is to try to accomplish as much as Mrs.

225
00:20:13,070 --> 00:20:18,757
Conner intimated in her introductory remarks — the great influence between the Cross Creek

226
00:20:18,930 --> 00:20:21,290
Church and the Bethel Church.

227
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:26,650
WHITE: I wanted to include in a sort of a trilogy the Upper Buffalo Church

228
00:20:26,910 --> 00:20:32,730
too, because of the fact that James Dinsmore went to that church — there

229
00:20:32,730 --> 00:20:37,330
were elders there, his son and his grandson.

230
00:20:37,790 --> 00:20:44,630
James Dinsmore III, son of John, [married] Mary Margaret Lyell, daughter of Robert Lyell,

231
00:20:44,650 --> 00:20:47,943
and they were my great-grandparents.

232
00:20:48,810 --> 00:20:55,170
About 1830 this couple moved to a farm which John Dinsmore had purchased in

233
00:20:55,170 --> 00:20:57,190
Cross Creek Township near Cross Creek Village.

234
00:20:57,630 --> 00:21:04,710
And here in 1837, James Dinsmore III, grandson of James I, was elected a

235
00:21:04,710 --> 00:21:09,070
ruling elder, to serve the church there until his death in 1870.

236
00:21:09,810 --> 00:21:13,350
WHITE: When he went to the Cross Creek Church, it was being served as pastor

237
00:21:13,350 --> 00:21:18,530
by the Reverend John Stockton, and Stockton [was] pastor there during the rest of

238
00:21:18,950 --> 00:21:26,550
Dinsmore's life — John Stockton.

239
00:21:28,650 --> 00:21:35,470
WHITE: There was a pastor there and a word or two should be said about

240
00:21:35,470 --> 00:21:42,510
him. He belonged to the famous Stockton family who had come to the Washington,

241
00:21:42,670 --> 00:21:43,690
Pennsylvania, area.

242
00:21:45,470 --> 00:21:53,430
WHITE: In the late 1700s, early 1800s, John Stockton was

243
00:21:53,430 --> 00:21:58,070
a graduate of Washington College in the class of 1820.

244
00:21:58,750 --> 00:22:04,050
WHITE: When he came to be pastor of the Cross Creek Church, he brought with

245
00:22:04,050 --> 00:22:09,730
him not only his interest in the work of the kingdom, but also he

246
00:22:09,730 --> 00:22:14,790
was interested in the education of the young people of his, the young men,

247
00:22:14,790 --> 00:22:16,430
I should say, of his congregation.

248
00:22:18,810 --> 00:22:26,530
WHITE: In 1827, when he came to Cross Creek Church, the school systems of

249
00:22:26,530 --> 00:22:30,910
our area were not very well developed.

250
00:22:31,510 --> 00:22:38,030
The Public School Act of 1834 had not been passed, and so the best

251
00:22:38,030 --> 00:22:44,090
schools that they had in the various communities were the so-called subscription schools.

252
00:22:44,850 --> 00:22:52,190
WHITE: However, to supplement the subscription schools, plenty of communities were developing

253
00:22:52,190 --> 00:22:59,570
the so-called academies. And so John Stockton was pastor at the Cross Creek Church

254
00:22:59,570 --> 00:23:02,790
only one year when he decided the Cross Creek area needed an academy.

255
00:23:05,790 --> 00:23:12,950
WHITE: So he was instrumental in organizing the Cross Creek Academy in 1828, and that

256
00:23:13,230 --> 00:23:20,930
existed, [with] some interruptions, until the year 1895, when it became the Cross

257
00:23:20,930 --> 00:23:22,710
Creek Township High School.

258
00:23:23,110 --> 00:23:30,970
Soon after he came to Cross Creek and had the academy going, he

259
00:23:30,970 --> 00:23:38,230
looked around for the best young men from the college classes of Washington College

260
00:23:38,230 --> 00:23:45,210
at Washington, Jefferson College at Canonsburg, to find able young men to serve as

261
00:23:45,210 --> 00:23:47,230
teachers in the academy.

262
00:23:47,910 --> 00:23:54,290
WHITE: And among the fine young men who he secured to teach there — I

263
00:23:54,290 --> 00:23:58,830
think during the very early years, he must have been during the early years

264
00:23:58,830 --> 00:24:05,130
of the Cross Creek Academy — he employed the young man George Marshall as

265
00:24:05,130 --> 00:24:06,590
a teacher in the academy.

266
00:24:07,450 --> 00:24:12,210
WHITE: Mr. Marshall was a graduate of Jefferson College, the class of 1831.

267
00:24:12,770 --> 00:24:18,390
Well, outside of that, John Stockton had graduated from Washington College in 1820.

268
00:24:18,830 --> 00:24:21,670
It was a time when there was a great deal of competition between the

269
00:24:21,670 --> 00:24:22,070
two colleges.

270
00:24:27,430 --> 00:24:34,550
WHITE: You know, they were eventually united into the present Washington and Jefferson College, but

271
00:24:34,550 --> 00:24:37,850
during the years there was a great deal of rivalry.

272
00:24:38,410 --> 00:24:44,870
I think sometimes it was bitter too, but at least the graduates of the

273
00:24:44,870 --> 00:24:52,450
two institutions were liberal enough to recognize the goodness of the

274
00:24:52,450 --> 00:24:54,090
graduates of the other college.

275
00:24:54,250 --> 00:25:01,270
So John Stockton, I think, had no compulsion or no revulsion at all against

276
00:25:01,270 --> 00:25:05,690
the employment of George Marshall as a teacher in the Cross Creek Academy.

277
00:25:07,150 --> 00:25:12,743
WHITE: Well, the tenure of Marshall as a teacher there must have been very short.

278
00:25:14,560 --> 00:25:19,560
According to the history of Washington Presbytery, Mr.

279
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:20,440
Marshall was licensed to preach, I think, in the year 1830.

280
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:31,420
WHITE: And he was ordained to the ministry and became a pastor of the Bethel

281
00:25:31,420 --> 00:25:33,580
Church here in 1833.

282
00:25:34,060 --> 00:25:38,940
So I would give him maybe only two years at the most, not more

283
00:25:38,940 --> 00:25:42,920
than three, to have been connected with the Cross Creek Academy.

284
00:25:43,500 --> 00:25:49,420
But he did make one other affiliation with the Cross Creek country, which was

285
00:25:49,420 --> 00:25:56,620
a lifelong affiliation, in that he married Mary Lee,

286
00:25:56,820 --> 00:26:00,020
daughter of Hugh Lee of the Cross Creek family.

287
00:26:01,640 --> 00:26:06,380
WHITE: And as I say, he was ordained to the ministry here in 1833.

288
00:26:06,980 --> 00:26:11,640
He was its pastor, and he served until his death in 1872.

289
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:19,880
WHITE: During the years of his ministry here, I happen to know that a lasting

290
00:26:19,880 --> 00:26:23,100
friendship existed between the two men, Dr.

291
00:26:23,240 --> 00:26:30,060
John Stockton, who was, I suppose, about 10 years older than George Marshall.

292
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:36,360
Then the fact that George Marshall was married into the Lee family of Cross

293
00:26:36,360 --> 00:26:41,800
Creek — that meant that there was a tie with Cross Creek which never

294
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:43,780
was broken until Dr. Marshall's death.

295
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:53,200
WHITE: He died in 1872, and Dr. Stockton didn't die until about 10 years later.

296
00:26:56,770 --> 00:27:02,040
WHITE: He remained a firm friend of John Stockton and frequently returned to the Cross

297
00:27:02,040 --> 00:27:05,940
Creek area and asked to greet him at the Cross Creek Church.

298
00:27:06,540 --> 00:27:14,520
The notes I have show some of the times that George Marshall preached at

299
00:27:14,520 --> 00:27:16,280
the Cross Creek Church as well.

300
00:27:16,800 --> 00:27:24,220
On April 2nd, 1854, he preached on the text Exodus 15:26, which reads,

301
00:27:24,500 --> 00:27:27,860
“For I am the Lord that healeth thee.”

302
00:27:27,860 --> 00:27:35,820
On June 18, 1858, from the text 1 Thessalonians 5:6, “Therefore let

303
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:41,500
us not sleep, as do others, but let us watch and be sober.”

304
00:27:42,120 --> 00:27:49,840
WHITE: And then the weekend of September 23, 24, and 25, 1864, during the late

305
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:51,000
years of the Civil War.

306
00:27:51,640 --> 00:27:57,480
WHITE: Dr. Marshall spent that weekend in the Cross Creek community and assisted Dr.

307
00:27:57,660 --> 00:28:00,540
Stockton in conducting the communion service.

308
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:07,800
You know, the old communion service started usually on Friday with a preparatory service,

309
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:12,540
ended with a second preparatory service on Saturday afternoon.

310
00:28:13,440 --> 00:28:17,600
WHITE: The sacrament itself was administered on Sabbath morning.

311
00:28:18,280 --> 00:28:24,040
And then, what perhaps some of you do not know, the communion service

312
00:28:24,040 --> 00:28:31,840
continued into Monday morning, when a post-communion sermon was

313
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:36,360
preached by a pastor or by a visiting pastor.

314
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:44,480
So this weekend, on September 23, 1864, from the text of Hosea 10:12, Dr.

315
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:49,160
Marshall preached, “Sow yourselves in righteousness, and reap in mercy.”

316
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:57,780
WHITE: And on Saturday afternoon, Luke 15:10 — “Likewise I say unto

317
00:28:57,780 --> 00:29:03,390
you, there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one

318
00:29:03,390 --> 00:29:04,950
sinner that repenteth.”

319
00:29:05,910 --> 00:29:11,150
WHITE: According to the record, on Sunday morning — that was morning September 25th —

320
00:29:11,150 --> 00:29:13,750
he assisted Dr. Stockton with the sacrament.

321
00:29:14,510 --> 00:29:22,050
WHITE: On January 28th, 1866, preaching from Revelation 2:5 — “Remember therefore

322
00:29:22,050 --> 00:29:27,930
whence thou art fallen, and repent, and do the first works, or else I

323
00:29:27,930 --> 00:29:32,690
will come to thee quickly, and will remove thy candlestick out of his place,

324
00:29:32,810 --> 00:29:34,950
except thou repent.”

325
00:29:35,830 --> 00:29:38,930
WHITE: The last record I have of Dr.

326
00:29:38,930 --> 00:29:46,550
Marshall's preaching at Cross Creek was on June 23, 1867, which was indicated

327
00:29:46,550 --> 00:29:53,950
as the last day, when he preached from Jeremiah 8:22 — “Is there no

328
00:29:53,950 --> 00:29:57,850
balm in Gilead? Is there no physician there?”
